2. Research Findings
2.1 Survey and Interview Demographics (15 responses)
Age: mostly 18 to 20, with outliers at 16, 17, 25, 42, 43.
Gender: 10 male, 4 female, 1 prefer not to say.
Occupations: mostly students (Software Engineering, Psychology, etc.), plus a Senior
Software Engineer, a business owner, and a housewife.
Exercise frequency: 0 to 2 times a week (10 users), 3 to 4 times a week (4 users), more
than 5 times a week (1 user).
Lost track of progress: Yes (12), No (2).
Would pay for an automated solution: Maybe (10), No (4), Yes (1).
Reasons for losing track: managing family or company business mid workout; forgetting
to count reps or losing focus; distraction by music, videos, or sudden thoughts; cutting
workouts short due to errands or childcare.
2.2 Persona 2.0 Interviews (18 September 2026)
Interview structure: (1) motivation and obstacles, (2) current tracking methods and why,
(3) inconveniences of current solutions, (4) experiences losing track of progress, (5)
impact on motivation, (6) ideal solutions.
High Schooler Newbie (4 interviews)
Motivations: gain weight, aesthetics, peer confidence, sports prep.
Obstacles: low energy after school, procrastination loops, gym intimidation, no budget.
Tracking: memory, cousins, YouTube (no counting).
Wants: free "coach in pocket", automatic rep counting, visual progress ("you did more
than last week"), calorie calculators.
Fit Undergraduate (3 interviews)
Motivations: weight management, stress relief, budget constraints.
Obstacles: shifting class schedules, rhythm broken by manual typing in notes apps.
Tracking: free notes apps with tally marks, physical notebooks (often forgotten).
Wants: flexible and adaptive plans, visual tracking, zero subscription auto counters
(camera or wearable), simple weekly logs.
Corporate Lifter (2 interviews)
AtomicFit: Compiled Source Material for SRS
Page 2 of 16

Motivations: longevity, energy for family, doctor recommendations.
Obstacles: sudden meetings or on call work cut sessions short and make them feel
unproductive.
Tracking: smartwatches (passive metrics like heart rate, no manual reps), mental notes.
Wants: adaptive system that detects pauses, resumes seamlessly, and gives low effort
weekly summaries.
On-the-Go Mom (3 interviews)
Motivations: functional strength for childcare, claiming time for herself.
Obstacles: unpredictable childcare demands, chores, errands. Apps expecting 45
uninterrupted minutes fail her.
Tracking: physical planners or notebooks (often rushed, forgets to log).
Wants: fully automatic background tracker that handles fragmented sessions and
remembers exact stoppage points.
9-to-5 Gym Bros (2 interviews)
Motivations: aesthetics, longevity, confidence.
Obstacles: dislike of the "chore" of lifting, traffic, the start, chore, stop, guilt cycle.
Tracking: free apps (Caliber), Garmin watches. Breaks cause total loss of routine.
Wants: highly automated, low friction dynamic planner that adjusts to readiness and
energy after a break, peer support features.
2.3 Synthesis
Universal problems: no real or accurate tracking system; tracking methods constantly
break down; data loss leads to motivation loss.
Common problems: busy schedules, losing count due to interruptions, financial
constraints, motivation decay.
What users are looking for: automatic tracking (no manual input), free or low cost,
dynamic adaptation to schedule, progress visualization.
