3.3Three Chosen HMWQuestions and Crazy 8's Ideas
HMW 1: Record a set without stopping, typing, or remembering. Ideas: voice recording,
one tap log, auto detection (sensors), smartwatch tracking, camera recognition (AI
estimation), quick gesture (shake or tap), preset sets (auto prepare next set), automatic
workout timeline (background tracking).
HMW 2: Turn a long break into a fresh start. Ideas: "Welcome Back" stat reset choice,
Auto-Ramp-Down weights (Smart Ease-In), 3 minute re-ignition workout, Seasons episode
framing, Memory Lane wins recap, No-Judgment Diagnostic Quiz (energy check in),
"Pause" history log (retroactive rest blocks), fresh start theme or skin unlock.
HMW 3: Make a broken session still feel like it counted. Ideas: "Save Point" Resume Card,
weekly rep bucket visualizer, Micro-Session Auto-Classifier ("Power Blast"), lock screen
quick add counter, end of day rep collector notification, voice drop quick log, Flexi-Goal
adjuster, asynchronous set stitching (merges sets at midnight).
4. Value Proposition
4.1Three Core Categories
Category 1: Zero Friction Logging. Goal: quick, easy logging with little to no typing.
1. Smartwatch tracking and auto detection (phone or watch sensors detect reps).
2. Voice Quick Log (dictate "3 sets of 10 squats").
3. Lock Screen Quick Add (add reps without unlocking).
4. Preset Sets and One Tap Log (app remembers past metrics; user presses "Done").
Category 2: Asynchronous and Fragmented Workouts. Goal: make short or interrupted
workouts feel successful.
1. "Save Point" Resume Card (remembers exact stoppage point, e.g. Set 3, Rep 6).
2. Weekly Rep Bucket (aggregates all reps across the week; progress over perfection).
3. Asynchronous Set Stitching (combines morning and afternoon sets).
4. Micro-Session Auto-Classifier (adjusts goals for short workouts to validate effort).
Category 3: Psychological Reboot and Guilt Reduction. Goal: easier returns after a break.
1. Smart Ease-In / Auto-Ramp-Down (safely reduces weights by 15 to 20 percent after
weeks away).
2. "Pause" History Log (marks missed days as intentional "Recovery Phases").
AtomicFit: Compiled Source Material for SRS
Page 6 of 16

3. No-Judgment Diagnostic Quiz (short 3 minute reboot workouts based on current
energy).
4. Seasons / "Welcome Back" Reset (reframes long breaks as a new "Season").
4.2 Value Proposition Diagram
Customer jobs: log and track volume, measure progress, restart routine after a hiatus.
Pains: "failed session" feeling, logging friction, all or nothing mindset, guilt cycle.
Gains: cost efficiency, graceful adjustments, seamless continuation, every rep counts.
Pain relievers: Smart Ease-In Auto-Ramp-Down, "Recovery Phase" history converter.
Gain creators: weekly volume bucket, "Save Point" Resume Card, Zero Friction Logging
suite.
4.3 Convergence: Adaptive FlexEngine
Phase 1, Zero Guilt Check-In: a 14 plus day break triggers the No-Judgment Diagnostic
Quiz ("How is your energy today?"), intercepting guilt immediately.
Phase 2, Silent Calibration: based on inputs, Smart Ease-In dials back target weights
by 15 to 20 percent to prevent DOMS and build confidence.
Phase 3, Safety Net Validation: if a session is stopped early (e.g. 12 minutes in), the
Micro-Session Auto-Classifier retitles it "Power Blast", marks it "Achieved", and adds
the reps to the weekly bucket.
5. Core Features (detailed)
5.1 RepTracker
Why essential: users who hate typing mid set lose rhythm with text inputs. This feature
removes typing overhead while keeping logging instant and dependable.
User value: rhythm preservation (no sub menus or keyboards while out of breath); instant
validation (single tap gives visual feedback); low cognitive load (app anticipates actions
from past data).
Concept:
Pre-populated fields: weight and rep inputs auto filled in gray text with values from the
last session (or Diagnostic Quiz calibrations).
One tap log: a large green [DONE] button logs the set instantly.
AtomicFit: Compiled Source Material for SRS
Page 7 of 16

Quick adjustment: [+] and [-] steppers adjust counts with single taps instead of a
keypad.
5.2 Save Feature
Why essential: life is unpredictable (children, classes, lack of time). Traditional apps mark
interrupted workouts "Incomplete", triggering a "didn't count" mindset.
User value: asynchronous workout support (workouts in pieces across a day); guilt
elimination (progress stored like a video game save state); frictionless re entry (opening
the app returns the user to where they stopped).
Concept:
1. Background cache: active state (Exercise ID, Set Number, Reps, Timestamp) cached
locally in real time.
2. Auto pause detection: transitions into a Save Point if backgrounded or idle for more
than 15 minutes.
3. Resume widget: prominent home screen widget, "Paused at Set 3, Rep 6. [Resume] or
[Bank Completed Reps]."
5.3 Diagnostic Quiz
Why essential: returning after a multi week break brings guilt (broken streaks) and
physical failure (lost conditioning). The quiz intercepts these barriers before the user
reaches the gym floor.
User value: psychological clean slate (no shaming notifications or missed badges on
return); personalized expectation setting; preventative safety (scales volume and
intensity down to prevent severe DOMS).
Concept:
1. Hiatus trigger: fires automatically if days_since_last_workout is greater than or
equal to 14 days.
2. 2 tap assessment: "Welcome back! How is your energy level today?" Options: [Low]
[Medium] [Ready to Push].
3. Smart Calibration (Auto-Ramp-Down): scales target weights down 15 to 20 percent and
reduces sets for Low or Medium inputs, framed as "Optimal Re-Entry Load".
4. Immediate action: transitions to a short, manageable workout plan built on the input.
