7. User Journeys
1. On-the-Go Mom. Need: keep her place when pulled away mid set, because a paused set
feels like failing. Flow: open app, check for a saved workout (yes: start saved plan; no: start
quick default plan), log sets. If interrupted mid set, the pause point is auto saved. If she
resumes the same day, she continues the workout. If not, the session counts as achieved
through the auto classifier. If not interrupted, the workout finishes and saves.
2. The Fit Undergrad. Need: log reps without touching anything, because typing breaks
rhythm and one lost count makes the session feel wasted. Flow: start workout, set phone
down. With a smartwatch: hands free logging by voice or tap. Without: manual phone
logging. If he loses count, he taps the smartwatch to log reps or sets. Session finishes and
auto saves.
3. The 9-5 Gym Bro. Need: restart after a break without feeling like he is starting over.
Flow: open app after hiatus. If the hiatus is under 14 days, skip the quiz and use regular
weight. If over 14 days, show the No-Judgment Quiz. High energy gives regular weight;
Low or Medium gives a light ramp down. Complete the session and finish feeling content.
4. High Schooler Newbie. Need: know what to do next without a trainer, because YouTube
gives no structure or count. Flow: open app unsure what to do. First workout: onboarding
tour explaining the sections. Otherwise: suggest the next exercise. If he does not know
the exercise, he checks the GIF demo, mimics the move, and logs reps; if he knows it, he
performs and logs directly. If the session is cut short, the auto classifier caches the partial
session. Otherwise it is fully completed. Saved to history.
8. MVP Scope
8.1TaskBreakdown Approach
Components assigned: Design, Frontend, Sensor and Voice logic, Backend, Data,
Analytics, QA.
Workflow:
Design comes first.
Sensor logic and backend run in parallel (no inter dependencies).
Frontend and backend run in parallel, sharing only the API contract.
Data modeling happens alongside the backend.
Analytics is layered on once the core flow exists.
AtomicFit: Compiled Source Material for SRS
Page 10 of 16

QA comes last, but test cases are written early.
Reuse to avoid duplication:
1. One "save a set" API for rep counting, voice logging, and lock screen add.
2. One session data model for Micro Sessions and Set Stitching.
3. One preview and confirm UI pattern for Quiz and Stitch.
4. One analytics event schema and shared QA cases.
8.2Epic:EasyExerciseTracking and Re-engagement
Goal: track via hands free, voice, and lock screen controls to preserve rhythm; re engage
through non diagnostic quizzes, micro sessions, and set stitching to prevent guilt.
Story 1: Automatic Rep Counting. As a lifter, I want the app to count my reps
automatically so I don't have to touch my phone mid set.
Design: workout screen, live counter, start and stop states, error states.
Frontend: live counter component, manual +/- buttons.
Sensors: accelerometer and gyroscope data, smoothing filter, peak counting.
Backend: save a set API, offline save and sync.
QA: compare auto count with real count across 5 exercises.
Story 2: Voice Logging. As a lifter, I want to log reps by voice so I can record a set while my
hands are busy.
Design: mic button, listening indicator, confirm card.
Frontend: permission request, confirm and edit card.
Voice logic: speech to text, phrase parsing (e.g. "10 reps squat"), retry handling.
Backend: reuse the save set API.
QA: test in a noisy gym with different accents.
Story 3: Lock Screen Quick Add. As a lifter, I want to add a rep or set from my lock screen
so I never need to unlock my phone.
Design: lock screen widget layout.
Frontend: widget with +1 rep and +1 set, live count sync.
Backend: immediate save to prevent data loss.
QA: close the app mid set and verify the count survives.
AtomicFit: Compiled Source Material for SRS
Page 11 of 16

Story 4: Non-Diagnostic Quiz. As a busy lifter, I can take a short non diagnostic quiz about
my target, energy, and motivation so the app can adjust my program.
Design: prompt, question screens, preview screen.
Frontend: modal or banner, quiz flow, program preview UI.
Backend: 7 day inactivity trigger, rule engine for adjustment.
Data and analytics: quiz attempt tracking, funnel dashboards.
QA: rule matrix logic, accessibility testing.
Story 5: Micro-Sessions. As a lifter who needs to leave mid workout, I want the completed
portion saved as a micro session.
Design: bottom sheet exit intercept, micro session badge.
Frontend: exit interception, history UI update, resume UI.
Backend: session state tracking, micro session save and resume APIs.
Data and analytics: session type enum, save and discard rates.
QA: background app kill, offline sync, streak edge cases.
Story 6: Set Stitching. As a lifter, I want to combine multiple sessions into one stitched
session.
Design: selection mode, preview card, stitched card.
Frontend: multi select history, preview and unstitch UI.
Backend: eligibility service, stitch preview, stitch service, unstitch APIs.
Data and analytics: stitched_session table, parent and child IDs.
QA: cross midnight tests, prevent stats double counting.
Why these stories: the MVP tackles the two main reasons users drop out: in workout
friction (auto rep counting, voice logging, lock screen quick add) and post lapse guilt (non
judgment quiz, micro sessions, set stitching).
