2. Quiz naming and content: called "Diagnostic Quiz" and "No-Judgment Diagnostic
Quiz" in most sources, but "Non-Diagnostic Quiz" in the MVP. The concept describes a
2 tap energy check (Low, Medium, Ready to Push), while the wireframe shows a 10
question flow starting with fitness target. Decide which is the intended behavior.
3. Persona naming: research uses "Corporate Lifter" and "9-to-5 Gym Bros"; the journeys
use "9-5 Gym Bro"; the SRE deck uses six named personas (Kevin, Joshua, Cindy,
Jamie, Tony, Sarah). Choose one set for the SRS stakeholder section.
4. Ramp down values: all sources say 15 to 20 percent reduction in target weight; confirm
whether sets are also reduced and by how much.
5. Auto pause threshold: Save Feature specifies 15 minutes of idle or background time;
confirm this against the Micro-Session exit intercept in MVP Story 5.
6. Platform and sensors: MVP assumes phone sensors (accelerometer and gyroscope),
voice input, and a lock screen widget; smartwatch support appears in the journeys and
ideation. Confirm target platforms (iOS, Android) and whether smartwatch is in or out
of MVP scope.
7. Cost: research shows users want free or low cost; only 1 of 15 would definitely pay. The
pricing or monetization model is not defined in any source.
